For a building rectangle [l, r] x [0, h], the set of sun-center positions x_s
for which the building intersects the sun disk is [l - R, r + R] while the
vertical overlap condition requires h >= 0; with silhouettes against the horizon,
the "last blocked moment" is the rightmost x for which every ray from the sun to
the horizon is blocked by some building. This reduces to computing the rightmost
covered point among intervals induced by buildings that can block the sun.

Because the original statement is truncated and no exact input format is visible,
the implementation below uses a common format:
multiple datasets:
R N
then N lines: l r h
terminated by EOF.

It computes the rightmost point covered by the union of intervals [l-R, r+R]
for all buildings, assuming any building contributes. If there are no buildings,
answer is "0.00".

If your judge uses a different exact format/geometry condition, the code may need
adjustment once the full statement is known.
*/

